      content:
          '**Diagnosis:** 3 cm left femoral limb length discrepancy (LLD) due to post-traumatic malunion, with compensatory gait abnormalities, secondary hip pain, and functional lumbar scoliosis.\n\n**Radiographic Leg Length Measurement Methods:**\n- **Scanogram (teleoroentgenogram):** Gold standard — single long-cassette AP radiograph of both lower extremities with ruler. Measures true bony length from femoral head to ankle. Confirms 3.0 cm discrepancy.\n- **CT scanogram:** Most accurate method if available, lower radiation than full CT. Can identify specific location of shortening (femur vs tibia).\n- **Block test:** Clinical correlation — standing with calibrated blocks under short leg until pelvis levels. Functional LLD may differ from anatomic LLD due to joint contractures or foot deformities.\n\n**Compensatory Biomechanics:**\n- **Short leg side:** Equinus positioning (toe-walking), hip hiking (quadratus lumborum overuse), increased energy expenditure, gluteus medius overload (explains hip pain)\n- **Long leg side:** Knee flexion posture, increased ground reaction forces, predisposition to knee OA over time\n- **Spinal compensation:** Functional scoliosis with convexity toward short side, asymmetric facet loading (explains new back pain), risk of progressive structural changes if uncorrected during remaining growth\n\n**Scoliosis Risk Assessment:**\n- Current scoliosis is **functional** (corrects with leveling pelvis) — not yet structural\n- However, persistent asymmetric loading during remaining 1.5-2 years of growth could lead to structural scoliotic changes\n- Need standing full-spine AP radiograph to measure Cobb angle and monitor\n- If Cobb angle >10 degrees, more aggressive correction warranted',
