          '**Q: The orthopedic surgeon recommends limb lengthening with an external fixator (Ilizarov technique) estimating 3 months in the fixator followed by 3 months of rehabilitation. The patient asks your opinion. What is your role?**\n\n**A:** As the physiatrist, your role is to provide comprehensive rehabilitation counseling for the surgical decision, not to recommend for or against surgery:\n\n**Surgical Options Comparison:**\n- **Distraction osteogenesis (Ilizarov/Taylor Spatial Frame):** Can achieve 3 cm lengthening reliably. Requires external fixator for ~3 months (1 mm/day distraction). Complications: pin site infections (20-30%), joint stiffness, muscle contracture, refracture, nerve injury. Prolonged rehabilitation.\n- **Intramedullary lengthening nail (PRECICE/STRYDE):** Newer technology — eliminates external fixator. Magnetic-driven internal nail for gradual lengthening. Lower infection risk, better cosmesis, but higher cost and limited availability. May not be appropriate if existing IM nail is in place.\n- **Contralateral epiphysiodesis:** If significant growth remains, surgically arresting growth on the long (right) side could equalize over time. Less invasive but results in shorter overall stature and requires precise growth prediction. Timing-dependent.\n\n**Rehabilitation Perspective to Share:**\n- Post-surgical rehabilitation is intensive: ROM preservation during distraction, progressive weight-bearing, strengthening, gait retraining\n- Timeline: realistically 6-9 months from surgery to full athletic return\n- This will mean missing an entire basketball season and significant school disruption\n- Psychological readiness matters — the patient and BOTH parents need to be aligned\n\n**Conservative Alternative Review:**\n- A 3 cm shoe lift is a permanent, effective, non-surgical option\n- Modern shoe modifications can be cosmetically acceptable (internal lift + slight external build-up)\n- No surgical risks, no downtime, immediate correction\n- Limitation: requires lifelong use, limits shoe choices, may not fully address the adolescent\'s psychosocial concerns',
