          '**Q: After 6 weeks with the shoe lift, his gait has improved but he is still not wearing it consistently, especially at school. His back pain is better on days he wears it but returns on days he does not. What do you do?**\n\n**A:** Adherence is the primary challenge in adolescent orthotic management:\n\n1. **Identify specific barriers:** Have a direct conversation WITH the patient (not just parents). Common barriers: cosmetic appearance ("it looks weird"), doesn\'t fit in preferred shoes (basketball shoes, casual shoes), peers noticed and commented, discomfort/heaviness, inconvenience of having only one modified shoe.\n\n2. **Address each barrier:**\n- **Cosmetic:** Refer to a skilled pedorthist who can modify popular brand-name athletic shoes with internal lifts that are virtually invisible. Some companies specialize in cosmetically appealing shoe modifications for teens.\n- **Multiple pairs:** Prescribe modifications for at least 3 pairs — school shoes, basketball shoes, casual shoes. Cost is justified by medical necessity.\n- **Peer concern:** Ask if a trusted friend knows. Sometimes having one friend who understands eliminates anxiety. Frame the lift as sports equipment, not a medical device — "professional athletes use custom shoe modifications all the time."\n\n3. **Motivational approach:** Link adherence to his stated goal of returning to basketball. "Every day without the lift is a day your back and hip are taking damage that will delay your return to the court."\n\n4. **Objective monitoring:** Schedule a follow-up standing spine film to show him the objective improvement with the lift. Adolescents respond to visual evidence.\n\n5. **Consider alternatives:** If he absolutely refuses external modification, discuss surgical options more seriously. A 16-year-old who will not wear a lift for 60+ more years of life may be a reasonable surgical candidate.',
