          '**Q: His divorced parents disagree openly in front of you. Mom says "No surgery — he\'s been through enough." Dad says "Just fix it permanently — a shoe lift is a crutch." The patient is silent. How do you handle this?**\n\n**A:** This requires managing parental conflict while centering the adolescent patient:\n\n**Step 1 — Acknowledge both perspectives:** "I can see you both want what is best for your son, and I respect that you each have a strong opinion. Both of your perspectives have merit — there are valid reasons to consider either approach."\n\n**Step 2 — Redirect to the patient:** "But before we continue, I want to hear from the person this actually affects the most." Turn to the patient: "This is your body and your life. What matters most to you right now? What questions do you have?"\n\n**Step 3 — Establish medical framework:** "Let me take the family dynamics out of this for a moment and share the medical facts:\n- A 3 cm discrepancy requires correction — doing nothing is not a safe option because of what is happening to his spine.\n- A shoe lift works immediately, has no surgical risks, and is the first-line treatment.\n- Surgery is a valid option for definitive correction but has real risks and a 6-9 month recovery.\n- These are not mutually exclusive — we can start with the lift and pursue surgery later if needed.\n- There is no emergency — we have time to make the surgical decision carefully."\n\n**Step 4 — Propose a structured plan:** "Here is what I recommend: We start the shoe lift today because his spine needs protection now. We schedule a follow-up in 3 months with new spine films. At that point, we will have objective data about how the lift is working. If the lift resolves the scoliosis and he is functioning well, surgery may not be needed. If the scoliosis is progressing or he cannot tolerate the lift, surgery becomes a stronger consideration. This gives us time, data, and does not close any doors."\n\n**Step 5 — Establish collaborative decision-making:** "I want all three of you — and both parents need to agree — before any surgical decision is made. I am happy to have a separate meeting with each of you if that would be more comfortable, and I can also arrange a joint meeting with the orthopedic surgeon so everyone hears the same information."',
